Title = {Exploring the structural landscape of DNA maintenance proteins},
Journal = {NATURE COMMUNICATIONS},
Year = {2024},
Volume = {15},
Number = {1},
Month = {SEP 5},
Abstract = {Evolutionary annotation of genome maintenance (GM) proteins has
   conventionally been established by remote relationships within protein
   sequence databases. However, often no significant relationship can be
   established. Highly sensitive approaches to attain remote homologies
   based on iterative profile-to-profile methods have been developed.
   Still, these methods have not been systematically applied in the
   evolutionary annotation of GM proteins. Here, by applying
   profile-to-profile models, we systematically survey the repertoire of GM
   proteins from bacteria to man. We identify multiple GM protein
   candidates and annotate domains in numerous established GM proteins,
   among other PARP, OB-fold, Macro, TUDOR, SAP, BRCT, KU, MYB (SANT), and
   nuclease domains. We experimentally validate OB-fold and MIS18 (Yippee)
   domains in SPIDR and FAM72 protein families, respectively. Our results
   indicate that, surprisingly, despite the immense interest and long-term
   research efforts, the repertoire of genome stability caretakers is still
   not fully appreciated.
   Here the authors use computational methods for sequence and structure
   analysis of genome maintenance proteins to catalog and identify genome
   maintenance families across species. This allows to identify several
   genome maintenance family members.},
